Description: Expect CET rather than MET in the %Z tests.
 The IANA time zone data changed so that the MET zone (which the tests
 use) behaves as CET; the abbreviation reported for it is now CET, so
 these two assertions in Tests/base/NSCalendarDate/test02.m fail with
 recent tzdata (2024a and later).
 This mirrors upstream commit 46d19b5dd5 ("Updates for changes to IANA
 time zone data."), which changes the expected value from MET to CET for
 the same reason; the upstream commit also adds the GNUSTEP_BUILTIN_TZ
 mechanism, which is not needed here.
Author: lichenggang <lichenggang@uniontech.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-10-21
---

--- gnustep-base.orig/Tests/base/NSCalendarDate/test02.m
+++ gnustep-base/Tests/base/NSCalendarDate/test02.m
@@ -169,7 +169,7 @@
        "%%Y format works in description");
   
   PASS([[myBirthday descriptionWithCalendarFormat: @"%Z" 
-                     locale: myLocale] isEqualToString: @"MET"],
+                     locale: myLocale] isEqualToString: @"CET"],
        "%%Z format works in description");
   
   PASS([[myBirthday descriptionWithCalendarFormat: @"%z" 
@@ -193,7 +193,7 @@
        "%%H%%%%%%M%%%%%%S format works in description");
   
   PASS([[myBirthday descriptionWithCalendarFormat: @"%H:%M (%Z)" 
-                     locale: myLocale] isEqualToString: @"13:00 (MET)"],
+                     locale: myLocale] isEqualToString: @"13:00 (CET)"],
        "%%H%%M format works in description");
   
   PASS([[myBirthday descriptionWithCalendarFormat: @"%R" 
